Change: Rotate owncloud.log at 100 MB by default

`log_rotate_size` came with the value `false`, so an installation that was never
explicitly configured for log rotation wrote a single owncloud.log that only the
disk could stop. In the field that produced log files well past 40 GB, on
machines where the first sign of trouble was a full partition.

The default is now 104857600 (100 MB). Rotation still happens in the cron job as
before: owncloud.log becomes owncloud.log.1 and an older owncloud.log.1 is
overwritten, so the log files together take at most twice that value.

Setting `log_rotate_size` to `false` or `0` still switches rotation off
completely - installations that hand their log files to logrotate or a
comparable tool keep working unchanged, and nothing about an existing explicit
setting is touched on upgrade.

Two values that used to behave oddly were tidied up on the way: `true` now means
"rotate at the default size" rather than "rotate on every single cron run", and a
size written as text in a hand-edited config.php ('100 MB') is parsed instead of
being compared as a string.
